  "design_note": "A non-LLM instrument (a plain statistical classifier trained on the evidence-privileged reference labels) is asked to predict those labels from the window text under grouped cross-validation by citing paper. If a supervised model with direct access to the answers cannot beat a constant-majority baseline, the deficit is a property of the text, not of the LLM instrument class. Provenance is run as a contrast: it is the target where the LLM panel pulls clearly ahead of the cheap statistical instrument, so it shows the comparison can detect an instrument-class gap when the text carries signal -- and finds none on the verdict.",
  "interpretation": {
    "caveats": [
      "Intervals are paper-clustered bootstraps; with a rare minority class and clustering, delta-accuracy CIs are wide even for the LLM panel on provenance.",
      "There is a little text signal on the verdict: structural and LLM instruments both beat the baseline on macro-F1 (they recover some 'undefined' windows), but not on accuracy.",
      "The single 'narrows' reference label falls outside the deployed-scored set, so the verdict evaluation is effectively binary (survives vs undefined)."
    ],
    "headline": "A non-LLM statistical instrument, trained directly on the reference labels, does no better than the deployed LLM panel on the verdict and no better than a constant baseline on accuracy; the verdict ceiling is therefore in the text, not in the LLM instrument class.",
    "provenance": {
      "any_supervised_beats_baseline": false,
      "baseline_accuracy": 0.794,
      "llm_accuracy": 0.906,
      "llm_minus_structural_accuracy": 0.124,
      "n_papers": 61,
      "n_windows": 170,
      "statement": "On provenance (N=170, 61 papers) the LLM panel reaches 0.906 accuracy, a +0.124 point lead over the structural instrument (0.782); the statistical instruments do not beat the baseline. So the statistical instrument is a conservative lower bound on extractable signal, yet it matches the LLM panel on the verdict while trailing it here -- the LLM's extra capacity buys a provenance lead but no verdict lead.",
      "structural_accuracy": 0.782
    },
    "verdict": {
      "any_estimator_beats_baseline_on_accuracy": false,
      "baseline_accuracy": 0.772,
      "n_papers": 48,
      "n_windows": 114,
      "statement": "On the disputed verdict (N=114 windows, 48 citing papers) no instrument -- not the deployed LLM panel, not TF-IDF, not the structural model -- significantly beats the constant-majority baseline on accuracy: every 95% CI on the delta-accuracy includes 0. The non-LLM structural instrument matches the LLM panel (accuracy 0.833 vs 0.816; macro-F1 0.753 vs 0.782), and both beat the baseline on macro-F1 by a similar margin (structural +0.317, CI [0.055, 0.394]). Bag-of-words carries no cross-paper signal (delta-accuracy 0.0). Because a different instrument class reaches the same ceiling, the ceiling is a property of the text, not a special failure of the LLM instrument class.",
